UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 1: Colour: primary, secondary and mixing', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Red, yellow and blue are the primary colours in paint. You cannot make them by mixing other paint colours. Mixing two primaries makes a secondary colour: red and yellow make orange, yellow and blue make green, and blue and red make purple. Adding white makes a lighter tint and adding a tiny amount of a dark colour makes a darker shade. Add small amounts of the darker colour a little at a time, because it is strong.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou have only red, yellow, blue and white paint. How would you make a bright orange for a pumpkin and a pale purple for a flower? Write the colours you would mix and the amounts in parts.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a time. Writing the parts means you can make the same colour again.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they need and test on scrap paper first.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a colour wheel with the three primary colours and three secondary colours using paint, crayons or coloured pencils. Under each secondary colour write its recipe. Then mix one tint and one shade of your favourite colour and name each.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Red, yellow and blue are the primary colours in paint. You cannot make them by mixing other paint colours. Mixing two primaries makes a secondary colour: red and yellow make orange, yellow and blue make green, and blue and red make purple. Adding white makes a lighter tint and adding a tiny amount of a dark colour makes a darker shade. Add small amounts of the darker colour a little at a time, because it is strong.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a colour wheel with the three primary colours and three secondary colours using paint, crayons or coloured pencils. Under each secondary colour write its recipe. Then mix one tint and one shade of your favourite col Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a time. Writing the parts means you can… Reasoning: I used a primary colour by attending to a secondary colour rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they… Correction: Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a primary colour.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they need and test on scrap paper first. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a primary colour on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a primary colour, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a… This matches the lesson skill for a primary colour.","options":["Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a…"],"question":"You have only red, yellow, blue and white paint. How would you make a bright orange for a pumpkin and a pale purple for a flower? Write the colours you would mix and the amounts in parts. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they… That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of a secondary colour with clear evidence.","Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they…","Red, yellow and blue are the primary colours in paint. You cannot make them by mixing other paint colours. Mixing two primaries makes a secondary colour: red…","Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a primary colour?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a secondary colour, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a secondary colour used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising a primary colour. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little a