_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Make a colour wheel with the three primary colours and three secondary colours using paint, crayons or coloured pencils. Under each secondary colour…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a primary colour?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a primary colour on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a time. Writing the parts means you can… Reasoning: I used a primary colour by attending to a secondary colour rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a primary colour, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they… Correction: Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a primary colour.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w02-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a primary colour.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use a primary colour with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a secondary colour and explain why it matters.","I can practise colour mixing independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a tint needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Red, yellow and blue are the primary colours in paint. You cannot make them by mixing other paint colours. Mixing two primaries makes a secondary colour: red and yellow make orange, yellow and blue make green, and blue and red make purple. Adding white makes a lighter tint and…","options":["Red, yellow and blue are the primary colours in paint. You cannot make them by mixing other paint colours. Mixing two primaries makes a secondary colour: red and yellow make orange, yellow and blue make green, and blue and red make purple. Adding white makes a lighter tint and adding a tiny amount of a dark colour makes a darker shade. Add small amounts of the darker colour a little at a time, because it is strong.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a primary colour?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ana wants a leaf green. She puts yellow on her palette first, then adds a small dab of blue. The mix is bright green. For a darker green for shadows she adds a bit more blue. For a pale spring green she adds white. She wrote the recipe: 3 yellow + 1 blue = light green.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou have only red, yellow, blue and white paint. How would you make a bright orange for a pumpkin and a pale purple for a flower? Write the colours you would mix and the amounts in parts.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a time. Writing the parts means you can make the same colour again.","explain":"Red, yellow and blue are the primary colours in paint. You cannot make them by mixing other paint colours. Mixing two primaries makes a secondary colour: red and yellow make orange, yellow and blue make green, and blue and red make purple. Adding white makes a lighter tint and adding a tiny amount of a dark colour makes a darker shade. Add small amounts of the darker colour a little at a time, because it is strong.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they need and test on scrap paper first. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Mixing primaries to make secondaries.","caption":"Two primaries make one secondary.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Red + yellow</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">makes orange</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Yellow + blue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">makes green</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ana wants a leaf green. She puts yellow on her palette first, then adds a small dab of blue. The mix is bright  Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ana wants a leaf green. She puts yellow on her palette first, then adds a small dab of blue. The mix is bright  Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a primary colour?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ana wants a leaf green. She puts yellow on her palette first, then adds a small dab of blue. The mix is bright green. For a darker green for shadows she adds a bit more blue. For a pale spring green she adds white. She wrote the recipe: 3 yellow + 1 blue = light green. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Tint and shade.","caption":"Add a little at a time.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Add white</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">a lighter tint</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Add dark</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">a deeper shade</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a primary colour?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they need and test on scrap paper first. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Ana wants a leaf green. She puts yellow on her palette first, then adds a small dab of blue. The mix is bright green. For a darker green for shadows she adds a bit more blue. For a pale spring green she adds white. She wrote the recipe: 3 yellow + 1 blue = light green.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a time. Writing the parts means you can make the same colour again.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they need and test on scrap paper first. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a primary colour?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRed, yellow and blue are the primary colours in paint. You cannot make them by mixing other paint colours. Mixing two primaries makes a secondary colour: red and yellow make orange, yellow and blue make green, and blue and red make purple. Adding white makes a lighter tint and adding a tiny amount of a dark colour makes a darker shade. Add small amounts of the darker colour a little at a time, because it is strong. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 1: Colour: primary, secondary and mixing","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Drawing, line, shape and colour","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Ana wants a leaf green. She puts yellow on her palette first, then adds a small dab of blue. The mix is bright green. For a darker green for shadows she adds a bit more blue. For a pale spring green she adds white. She wrote the recipe: 3 yellow + 1 blue = light green.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Mixing primaries to make secondaries.","caption":"Two primaries make one secondary.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Red + yellow</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">makes orange</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Yellow + blue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">makes green</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Tint and shade.","caption":"Add a little at a time.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Add white</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">a lighter tint</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Add dark</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">a deeper shade</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w02-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
